---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Audio and camera in ScreenRecorder"
  description="Learn how ScreenRecorder handles desktop sound, microphone audio, and optional camera overlays."
>
  <article class="guide wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/docs/">← All documentation</a><div
      class="eyebrow"
    >
      INPUTS · 4 MIN READ
    </div><h1>
      Bring your voice<br /><span class="accent-word">into focus.</span>
    </h1><p class="guide-lead">
      Add the sound and camera context your recording needs. You choose which
      inputs to enable.
    </p><h2>Windows audio</h2><p>
      The Windows app can capture the default microphone and desktop output
      through WASAPI Loopback. It aligns both sources using capture timestamps,
      then mixes them into one stereo AAC track at 48 kHz / 192 kbps.
    </p><p>
      Desktop audio is mixed below the microphone to keep speech in the
      foreground. You cannot adjust the relative source levels after recording
      in a standard player, because they share a track.
    </p><h2>Microphone behavior</h2><p>
      Microphone auto-leveling and a soft peak limiter help manage level
      changes. The current app does not include voice noise suppression or a
      compressor; a close microphone and an appropriate Windows input level help
      with clear speech.
    </p><h2>Camera overlay</h2><p>
      Camera overlay is optional. The Windows preview uses a throttled RGB32/GDI
      path to avoid a second GPU composition pipeline. Fedora offers a camera
      picture-in-picture path and a separate live preview window when a camera
      is available.
    </p><h2>Fedora permissions</h2><p>
      Fedora screen and window capture uses the desktop portal. Select the
      source in the system prompt. Camera and monitor audio are opt-in, and
      availability depends on the devices and desktop session.
    </p><a class="text-link guide-next" href="/docs/troubleshooting/"
      >Next: troubleshooting →</a
    >
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
